401	We say that an arrow type is \emph{applicable} to a type $A$
402	if $A$ is a subtype of the parameter type of the arrow type.
403	(If the parameter type has a varargs entry,
404	then $A$ must be a subtype of one of the types in the union
405	defined by the parameter type.)
406	
407	
408	Every arrow type is a subtype of \TYP{Any}.
409	No other type encompasses all arrow types.
410	A type parameter can be instantiated with an arrow type.
411	Arrow types cannot be extended by trait types.
412	Arrow types are covariant in their return type and exception types
413	and contravariant in their parameter type; that is,
414	arrow type ``\EXP{A \rightarrow B \KWD{throws}\:C}'' is a subtype of arrow type
415	``\EXP{D \rightarrow E \KWD{throws}\:F}'' if and only if:
416	\begin{itemize}
417	\item $D$ is a subtype of $A$,
418	\item $B$ is a subtype of $E$, and
419	\item for all $X$ in $C$, there exists $Y$ in $F$ such that
420	$X$ is a subtype of $Y$.
